---
/**
 * HELD, NOT PUBLISHED: the leading underscore keeps this page out of the
 * build until the page has its own review (operator, 2026-09-15). The
 * corner door, the footer link, and the cover exit that pointed here
 * are out with it. Rename to replicate.astro to publish.
 *
 * /replicate: the toolkit's yes to "could we have this where we live?"
 * (BR22). The reader who matters is someone organizing in another place:
 * the page gives the yes, the picture of their own copy, and one ask. The
 * developer steps come last. The corner door, the footer, and the cover's
 * exits point here.
 */
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const CONTACT_EMAIL = 'resiliencetoolkit@gocros.org';
const mailto = `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Bringing the toolkit to [your town]')}`;
const issueUrl =
  'https://github.com/000noyes/resiliencetoolkit.org/issues/new?template=bring-it-to-your-town.md&title=' +
  encodeURIComponent('Bring it to your town: ');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Bring it to your town | Resilience Hub Toolkit"
  description="Most of the Resilience Hub Toolkit works anywhere. What a copy keeps, what would change for your place, and how to ask for one."
>
  <article class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8 max-w-3xl">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <h1 class="text-hero font-semibold mb-4 text-foreground">Yes, you can bring it to your town.</h1>
      <p class="text-body-large text-text-secondary">
        This toolkit was written in Vermont after the floods of 2023 and 2024. Most of it works
        anywhere people organize to look after each other.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-10">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-title font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">What would change</h2>
        <p class="text-body text-foreground mb-3">
          A copy of this toolkit for your town works the same way this one does. It reads offline
          once you have opened it. Notes and checklists stay on your device. It prints as the
          workbook. Only a few parts are specific to Vermont, and your community would replace them:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-foreground">
          <li>The Vermont Town Directory. It is a card in the Downloads room and a row under Knowing Your Community in every contents list.</li>
          <li>The state map links in 0.1 and on the Map page.</li>
          <li>The flood story on the cover and on the About page.</li>
          <li>The local contacts in chapters 1.8, 1.9, 1.11, 2.1, 2.2, and 2.3.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-title font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">Ask us</h2>
        <p class="text-body text-foreground mb-3">
          <a href={mailto}>Write to {CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. Tell us where you are and what your group is
          doing, and we will answer with what a copy for your town would take.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body text-foreground">
          If you can set up a website yourself, the developer steps are below. You can also
          <a href={issueUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open a request on GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-title font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">For developers</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-body text-foreground">
          <li>
            Fork <a href="https://github.com/000noyes/resiliencetoolkit.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/000noyes/resiliencetoolkit.org</a>, then clone it and run it:
            <pre class="bg-muted rounded-lg p-4 mt-2 text-body-small overflow-x-auto"><code>git clone https://github.com/YOUR-ORG/resiliencetoolkit.org.git
cd resiliencetoolkit.org
pnpm install
pnpm dev</code></pre>
          </li>
          <li>
            The chapters live under <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">src/pages/modules/</code>.
            Their titles and order live in <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">src/data/contents.ts</code>.
            The workbook lives in <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">public/toolkit/</code>, and its
            page ranges live in <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">docs/source-specs/</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The build checks the chapter text against the workbook with
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">pnpm verify</code>. The check reads a committed
            extraction cache, so the host does not need PDF tools. If you replace the workbook, update its page
            ranges and refresh the cache locally with pdftotext, or set the build command to
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">pnpm astro build</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Cloudflare Pages is the tested host. The build command is
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">pnpm build</code> and the output folder is
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">dist</code>. The review-round API in
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">functions/</code> is optional. It needs a D1
            binding only if your copy runs review rounds. Other static hosts should work, but we have not tested them.
          </li>
          <li>
            The code is under the MIT License. The coalition sets the content license. Ask about it when you write.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <p class="text-body-small text-text-secondary border-t border-border pt-4">
        Built from the <a href="https://resiliencetoolkit.org">Resilience Hub Toolkit</a> by Community Resilience Organizations (CROs), Vermont.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
